From: =?UTF-8?q?John=20T=C3=B6rnblom?= <john.tornblom@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 20:34:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 19/28] radv/ps4: free a buffer's memory once the GPU is done
 with it

A destroyed buffer's direct memory went back to the pool at once. RADV
destroys buffers that submitted work still uses -- the queue's scratch,
GS and tessellation rings when it grows them, upload buffers -- and
relies on the amdgpu kernel keeping a buffer alive until every
submission that names it has run. Here the next allocation could take
the memory while the GPU still read or wrote it, or find the range
unmapped: a GPU page fault that ends the title. The DolphinPS4 RADV
port fixed the same thing (v02.18, "RADV relies on the kernel keeping
in-use buffers alive").

The memory of a destroyed buffer now waits, with its GPU mapping and its
place in the 32-bit window, for the submission that was the last when it
was destroyed (radv_ps4_queue_release). Submissions and allocations give
back what the GPU has finished with, and an allocation that finds no
room waits for the GPU to finish with the rest and tries again. A
placed mapping, which only the CPU uses, still goes at once.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Bb93U7uY5qVNuEcHDggEfE
